car tester michelle austyn maka realizes  that views can differ vastly on the  ideal city car for some it may be a  compact car for others a mini SUV this  Suns pulsar strikes the balance between  practicality and versatility this Sun  launched the Pulsar in 2015 it's  specially designed for the European  market and it's built in Barcelona  starting at 18,000 euros in Germany the  Pulsar is priced to appeal mainly to  young car buyers we tested a pulsar with  a gasoline-powered engine at the upper  end of the range it's 140 kilowatts can  push it from 0 to 100 km/h and 7.7  seconds the top of the range techni  variant commands a minimum price of  almost 27,000 euros in Germany  the Tecna includes for example LED  headlights as standard  the pulsars 2.7 meter wheelbase is the  longest of any car in the compact  segment that adds up to lots of space  inside  the high rear spoiler and the hint of an  underbody give the design a cutting edge  look top to bottom  folding the rear seat down adds another  thousand liters to the 385 litre cargo  space giving the Pulsar lots of room for  luggage if only it had a level floor in  the transporter configuration but it  doesn't  Michelle takes the compact out on the  town to test it and everyday traffic  situations  it's enough she notes that the color  ought to be an attention-getter and for  city driving a start/stop system is  indispensable the start/stop helps the  driver save fuel it automatically shuts  down the engine after its idle for a  certain time helping cut down a noise  pollution into the bargain  that's me the one thing Michelle doesn't  like about the interior is the center  console to her it looks as if someone  had plunked a huge block down here  instead of integrating it smoothly into  the dashboard otherwise the interior is  very high-quality with leather trim on  the steering wheel and seats as part of  the Tecna package the model we tested  was a six-speed stick shift but other  engines come in optional automatic  versions as well  and the spaciousness of the backseat can  set an example the pulsars technological  features are no less impressive the  360-degree display is a big help when  backing into a parking space with every  corner in full view the driver can avoid  many a scratch and ding in tight spots  the driver can access more assists by a  second display between the speedometer  and tachometer among them a lane  departure warning blind spot warning and  emergency brake assist the blind spot  warning helps the drivers stay on top of  things especially on the highway once  they've got the hang of it beginners and  Road veterans both can benefit from it  these safety features are part of the  overall techno line the lower-priced  equipment levels still deliver a  comprehensive city car on longer trips  to the nimble pulsar offers plenty of  scope for adventure  I invited Michelle sees the big  advantage of the Pulsar in its  spaciousness of course it's easier to  find a parking spot in a small car but  the Pulsar is also a viable alternative  for hobbyists with lots of equipment to  haul around such as personal associates  pure lighting fuel
 

